---
epr-meta-version: 1
id: epr-meta-tooling-governance
covers: dir-only
rules:
  - id: policies-yaml-review
    class: dispatch
    when: { write: "policies.yaml" }
    parameters:
      dispatch-agent: code-reviewer
      dispatch-prompt: >
        Audit the policy-registry edit for version-pin discipline: policy edits must add NEW
        version rows, never mutate an existing version in place (immutability is now
        contentHash-enforced); verify contentHash pins updated via epr-meta-pin.py; report
        violations
    why: >
      Meta-governance: the registry that governs governance gets agent review on every edit. The
      first live `dispatch` rule (constraint 4/T1e) — proof that the class works end-to-end
      (fires, witnesses, and directs a background agent) before other directories adopt it.
      `dispatch-agent`/`dispatch-prompt` are read by the resolver hook's dispatch side-channel;
      `class: dispatch` never blocks — it always resolves to `permit` plus the directive.
---

# `.claude/epr-meta/` — the policy registry's own governance

Dir-only (not `covers: subtree`): this directory holds the registry (`policies.yaml`), the
governor config (`governors.yaml`), and the compose recipes (`recipes.yaml`) — a flat leaf, not a
subtree with descendants worth claiming.

One live rule: every `policies.yaml` write dispatches a `code-reviewer` agent to audit version-pin
discipline (immutability is contentHash-enforced — see `epr-meta-pin.py --verify`). This is
deliberately `dispatch`, not `ask`: reviewing a registry edit is agent-self-grantable observation-
with-a-named-reviewer, not an authority narrowing. The repo-wide `governance-escalation-ladder`
policy (bound at the repo root — see `/.epr-meta/manifest.md`) is what actually gates a NEW
ask/deny rule appearing in a `.epr-meta` manifest; this dispatch rule is a separate, lighter-touch
concern (catch a bad pin/immutability violation early, every time).
